{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Take the Levites, etc.</b> Sometimes Scripture uses the language of taking (לְקִיחָה) and sometimes the language of giving (נְתִינָה), and this matter requires explanation. And further, what is the duplication where it says ״כִּי נְתוּנִים נְתוּנִים הֵמָּה לִי״ and afterward he contradicts his own words and says ״לָקַחְתִּי אוֹתָם לִי״? And it appears that from the very necessity of these difficulties he derives the interpretation in the Tanchuma (Beha'alotkha 8): ״קַח אֶת הַלְוִיִּם״ — take them for My name, for the name of dominion (שְׂרָרָה). And through this all the difficulties are resolved, for we find the language of taking (קִיחָה) used in reference to the exchange of the firstborn, as it is written: ״תַּחַת פֶּטֶר כָּל רֶחֶם וְגוֹ׳ לָקַחְתִּי אוֹתָם לִי״." "And the explanation of the matter is that the Levites were chosen for three things: two for servitude (שִׁעְבּוּד), and one for dominion (שְׂרָרָה). Two for servitude — that is, the subjugation of their bodies' labor to work and to carry, and the subjugation of the surrender of their bodies for atonement upon the children of Israel, as it is written: ״וָאֶתְּנָה אֶת הַלְוִיִּם נְתֻנִים לְאַהֲרֹן וּלְבָנָיו וְגוֹ׳ לַעֲבֹד אֶת עֲבֹדַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ — that is, the labor of carrying — ״וּלְכַפֵּר עַל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for the guilt of the people hangs upon their heads. Therefore it says: ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה בִּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל נֶגֶף״ and so forth, for through their joining the children of Israel are healed, for such is the measure — on the pattern of ״בִּקְרוֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ״ (Leviticus 10:3). And therefore it is stated: ״וְסָמְכוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת יְדֵיהֶם עַל הַלְוִיִּם״, and this semichah is to say: you are a sacrifice for the people and atonement upon them, in the manner we explained above in parashat Vayikra (1:4) regarding the verse ״וְסָמַךְ יָדוֹ עַל רֹאשׁ הָעֹלָה״, and as will be explained further on, with the help of YHWH, in parashat Pinchas (27:18) regarding the laying of Moses' hand upon Joshua. And these two things YHWH did not impose upon the Levites on account of the firstborn being disqualified. For is it really the measure that Tuviah sins and Zigud is punished?! (Pesachim 113a). Rather, these two things were imposed upon the Levites because Jacob, the grandfather of Israel, gave a tithe of all that he had, and of his sons he gave a tithe — Levi — for he had twelve sons, and Ephraim and Manasseh would be counted as Reuben and Simeon, making fourteen; remove from them four firstborn for the four mothers, ten remain, and of them he gave Levi. And since he said the doubled expression ״עַשֵּׂר אֲעַשְּׂרֶנּוּ״ and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketubot 67b) that one who distributes gives a fifth (חֹמֶשׁ), meaning two tithes, therefore he too gave the fifth (חֹמֶשׁ), and that is that he consecrated Levi to the Most High in these two things: to subjugate their bodies for the service of the holy and their souls for atonement — it is as if he set him apart twice, and that is the fifth (חֹמֶשׁ). And this is what it says: ״כִּי נְתוּנִים נְתוּנִים הֵמָּה לִי מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that is, the two acts of giving that we have mentioned, ״מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — the grandfather — so that the fifth (חֹמֶשׁ) would be set apart. And likewise in the verse ״וָאֶתְּנָה אֶת הַלְוִיִּם נְתֻנִים״ and so forth, he mentioned two acts of giving — וָאֶתְּנָה, נְתֻנִים — and immediately explained that these two acts of giving are ״לַעֲבֹד אֶת עֲבֹדַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, ״וּלְכַפֵּר עַל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and so forth. And he mentioned ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״ five times in this verse to indicate that the giving of the Levites, doubled, is the fifth (חֹמֶשׁ) from the children of Israel. And this is a fine and precious allusion." "But when Scripture mentions the language of taking (לְקִיחָה) — that the Holy One, blessed be He, took them on His own without Jacob's giving — that is a taking of dominion (שְׂרָרָה) which had belonged to the firstborn; they were disqualified and the dominion was given to the Levites. And regarding this it says: ״תַּחַת פֶּטֶר כָּל רֶחֶם בְּכוֹר כֹּל מִבְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָקַחְתִּי אוֹתָם לִי, וָאֶקַּח אֶת הַלְוִיִּם תַּחַת כָּל בְּכוֹר״ and so forth. And from here the master of the midrash learns to explain ״קַח אֶת הַלְוִיִּם״ as referring to dominion (שְׂרָרָה), and he learns the upper from the lower: just as the taking (קִיחָה) stated below is one of dominion, being dependent upon the exchange of the firstborn, so too the taking stated above is one of dominion. And the dominion is that all Israel are obligated to bring up to them the levy of terumot and ma'aserot, for therefore he called his name Levi — that he was accompanied (שֶׁלִּוָּהוּ) with gifts. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:27.222323904Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}